{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 14, :comments ["<b>And you shall say: I will set a king over me</b> — according to the opinion of our Sages (Sifrei Re'eh 67; Sanhedrin 20a), it is like <b>and say: I will set a king over me</b>, and it is a positive commandment, for it obligates us to say so after inheritance and settlement, in the language of (Deuteronomy 22:8) <b>and you shall make a parapet for your roof</b> and others. And Scripture mentioned <b>and you shall say</b>, because the commandment is that they shall come before the priests the Levites and to the judge, and they shall say to them: our desire is to set a king over us. And in my opinion, furthermore, this too is among his allusions to future events, for so it was when they asked for Saul, they said to Samuel (I Samuel 8:5): set a king over us to judge us like all the nations, and so it is written there (I Samuel 8:20): and we shall be also like all the nations, and our king shall judge us, etc. — for what reason would the Torah say in the commandment <b>like all the nations that are around me</b>, when Israel are not fit to learn from them or to emulate doers of iniquity? Rather, this is an allusion to the matter that would come to pass, and therefore the passage came in the intermediate tense, as I have already explained."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 17, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:51:22.010356320Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}